WEBVTT

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:01.980
ED CHARBENEAU: Hi.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.560
I'm Ed Charbeneau, principal
developer advocate here

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.580
at Progress Software and
10 time Microsoft MVP.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.470
And today I'll be giving
you an introduction

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.690
to retrieval
augmented generation.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.400
Over the next few
minutes we'll talk

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.170
about why you should use RAG,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.720
how retrieval augmented
generation compares

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.970
to context augmented generation,

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.220
and the key concepts
involved in RAG.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.790
I'll also share an introduction
to Progress Agentic RAG,

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.740
a RAG as a service platform.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.400
First let's start with why
retrieval augmented generation

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.870
is important today.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.810
Using retrieval augmented
generation is all

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.670
about making sense of your data.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.900
I gave a session back at
the M3 conference in 2016

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.750
where I talked about the
future of data storage

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.910
and how it relates
to machine learning.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.980
In that session it was estimated

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.110
that 44 zettabytes would have
been collected by the year 2020.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:59.010
Here we are in 2026
and we are generating

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.300
and collecting roughly
400 million terabytes

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.150
of data every day worldwide.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.850
That's about 0.4 zettabytes a
day or 147 zettabytes per year.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.350
So why does that matter?

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.220
Well, we need to make sense
of all that data we collect.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.790
And back in 2016 my
suggestion for doing this was

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.680
to use machine learning.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.900
The reason for that
is really simple.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.140
It's not humanly possible
to make use of all

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.160
that data we collect without
using some sort of AI.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.190
Today machine learning is
a commodity in the form

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:39.030
of large language models and the
task of sifting through data

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.010
for answers can be performed

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.610
by human operators assisted
by agentic systems.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:45.600
Before we can talk

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.510
about retrieval augmented
generation it's important

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.360
to talk about context
augmented generation.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.030
These core concepts will help
you understand the scale

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.790
of the technologies and
architectures involved.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.880
Context augmented generation
is where we add data

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.820
to the context window of
a large language model.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.520
For example, we might
take a file and upload it

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.280
in to the context window
so we can ask questions

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.620
about the data
contained in that file.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.440
An example of this can
be seen on YouTube.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.350
There's a Gemini button
on some YouTube videos

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.690
and when you click on that
button the transcript

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.750
of that video is loaded
in to the context window

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.790
of the large language model.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.210
That allows you to query
the video conversationally.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:31.290
Another form of context
augmented generation is cache

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.240
augmented generation.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.460
This involves taking
even more data

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.980
and storing it in vector memory.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.470
You can then search through
that memory structure,

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.590
pull out pertinent pieces
of information and data,

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.540
and insert those
in to the context

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.010
of the large language model.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.310
An example of this can be
seen inside of Copilot

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.960
within Visual Studio Code.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.930
Your agent has memory
of conversation.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.780
Your project files are
also loaded in to memory

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.990
as vector data for quick
semantic searching.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.750
And the large language
model has access to all

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.520
of this data in its context.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.030
When you ask it to perform
coding activities it can reach

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.940
in to its cache, retrieve
pertinent pieces of data,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.840
and use those to generate
code, answer questions,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.150
create summaries,
and so much more.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.240
Retrieval augmented
generation is another form

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.310
of context augmented generation.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.550
This time it's using a
vector database to store

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.870
and retrieve information

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:31.800
from large volumes using
semantic similarity.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.060
The retrieval portion

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.760
of retrieval augmented
generation is all

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.900
about finding useful information
through vector search.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.840
This is different from
traditional key word search.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.470
Instead of searching for exact
key words we use AI to search

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.980
for content with semantic
similar meaning.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.230
Once we find that
information we retrieve it

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.060
from the vector
database and place it

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.150
in to the context window of
the large language model.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.070
We then ask the
large language model

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:02.520
to generate a new response
using that information.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.480
We can also ask it to create
citations so users can trace

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.820
where the information
originated.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.510
Citations are an important
feature for tracing answers back

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.460
to their origin and providing

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.490
that the answer is grounded
in real knowledge.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.250
In other words, the
system can show receipts

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.730
that prove the answer
was not hallucinated.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:26.060
When we work with retrieval
augmented generation we use

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.120
vector databases to store
large amounts of data.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.060
We do this by ingesting
resources and passing them

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.990
through a vector
embedding model.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.050
That embedding model extracts
the meaning from the text

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.740
so we can perform searches
using semantic similarity.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.980
If a document is large enough we
may need to chuck that document

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.380
in to smaller bite sized pieces.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.200
That makes it easier to
ingest and store inside

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.670
of the vector database.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.480
Because RAG can store large
volumes of data it's perfect

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.820
for generative AI search.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.730
Generative AI search empowers
users by allowing them

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.940
to use natural language
to query large data sets

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.730
without memorizing
specific query syntax.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.860
It also helps make
sense of large volumes

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.950
of business data stored
in unstructured formats

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.940
such as websites,
PDFs, images, videos,

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.530
and many other resources.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.630
And it does this all
while querying them

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:19.620
as if they were coming

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.920
from a relational
database thanks to AI.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.600
And end to end RAG architecture
is actually pretty complex.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.300
It requires multiple
system components

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.570
that are often sourced
from multiple vendors.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.090
For example, you'll
need a user interface.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.310
You'll need embedding
and chat models.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.940
You'll need to have a data
strategy and document providers

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.330
for all of the documents I
talked about whether it's PDF,

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.510
Office files, video
images, etcetera.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:52.380
You'll need a provider
to translate those types

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.800
of documents in to text
so they can be embedded

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.900
in to the vector database.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.690
And then of course you'll
want to evaluate the quality

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:03.870
of the data coming in and
out of your RAG system.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.140
This requires a lot of
expertise to glue together all

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.760
of these different pieces

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.270
and the expertise involved
is software engineering,

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.000
data science, and AI experts.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.830
It's very difficult to scale

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.680
and it's also difficult
to predict cost.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.500
Some of these concerns were
cited in a recent article

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.110
by VentureBeat where
they suggested

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.510
that enterprises are
transitioning away

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.700
of their current RAG stacks.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.570
These were RAG systems
that were built in house

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.910
and didn't have the
fundamental knowledge

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.120
of agentic retrieval
augmented generation

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.660
where agents help
rerank search results

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.920
and evaluate system metrics as
data's ingested and retrieved.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.160
For a complete end to end
solution that solves all

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.600
of these problems for you
is Progress Agentic RAG.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.320
Progress Agentic RAG is a
rag as a service platform

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.880
that can ingest all sorts of
documents whether it's video,

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.090
audio, chat logs or
other information.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.850
Document providers are
already there in place

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.440
for you to ingest data.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.260
It makes sense of structured
and unstructured data

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.090
and there's agents
within the system

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:18.960
that can extract key
texts, tags, entities,

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.980
and generate embeddings all
with large language models.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:26.520
It has a hybrid search feature
that includes key word search,

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.400
semantic search,
and graph search.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.010
And all of that is
reranked by an agent.

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.470
It also has an embedded quality

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.990
and evaluation metric
known as REMi.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:40.860
This is an AI agent that
evaluates the system's stability

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.080
as data's ingested
and retrieved.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.770
And all of Progress Agentic RAG
can be managed through an easy

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:50.220
to use user interface that a
system administrator can log

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:55.620
in to to ingest and manage
data, orchestrate AI agents,

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.900
and check evaluation metrics.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.760
For projects that
require a quick turn

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.870
around time administrators can
use an HTML widget builder

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.420
to create robust
search experiences

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.540
that also include
citation and retrieval.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:14.220
For more complex scenarios such
as building custom agents,

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.750
custom user interfaces,

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:18.960
or complete application
architectures,

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.890
SDKs for.NET type
script in Javascript

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:27.180
and Python are available
as well as rest APIs.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.580
Let's take a look at a
customization scenario

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.640
and see how this would
work with.NET and Blazor.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.080
For this demo we're going

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.580
to create a business dashboard
using Progress Agentic RAG

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:42.900
and Blazor Server and connect
them using the C Sharp SDK.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:44.820
The financial dashboard
that we're going

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.300
to create will ingest
PDF financial statements

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.720
and extract pertinent
information from those PDFs

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.660
so we can generate
visualizations such as charts

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.150
and graphs and also
have conversations

00:08:57.150 --> 00:09:00.660
with the AI regarding
the data in those PDFs.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:04.620
The first step is to log in to
our Progress Agentic RAG system

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.570
and once we log in we're greeted

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.690
with our Progress
Agentic RAG dashboard.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:14.490
From here we can see metrics
on quality, storage,

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.460
and the last resources
that were ingested.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:21.600
From the upload tab we can
upload new resources in the form

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.840
of files, folders, links, text
resources, entire site maps,

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.820
and Q and A resources.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:33.210
The file resources can
be any office type.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:37.980
They can be videos, images,
and audio files like MP3s.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.200
I've already ingested
some resources here

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.990
and I can see them
in my resource list

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:45.570
and I have some
reports from some

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.700
of the big companies
of the world.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.330
We have Amazon,
Apple, and so on.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:54.900
So if I look at Apple's
SEC filings I can see

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:58.050
that I have a resource
here in my file field

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.690
and this is the
entire SEC filing

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.850
in PDF form that's been ingested

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.370
in to the Progress
Agentic RAG system.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:08.910
I also have some generated
fields and these fields are data

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:15.240
that has been extracted from
the PDF using AI agents

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.510
that are running on the
data in the background.

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:22.680
Those AI agents are configured
in our agent screen

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:26.700
within the Progress Agentic
RAG system and you can test

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:30.480
and evaluate those agents
within the UI here.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:33.840
This agent in particular
has been configured

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:39.090
to extract chart friendly data
from the unstructured files

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:41.280
that are being ingested
in to the system.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:45.870
This is a step that helps the
retrieval agent find the data

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:49.200
that we're looking for when we
start providing structured data

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.930
types to the system as queries.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:53.160
Once I've ingested data

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:57.060
in to the system I have a simple
search window that I can come in

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:01.380
and use to check my data
that has been ingested.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.660
So, for example, I
can ask questions

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:10.770
about what was Apple revenue
and I'll get a response

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:13.440
from the system that
Apple's total net sales

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.150
from 2024 were $391 million.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.820
This simple search can be
converted in to a widget

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:24.240
by hitting create widget and
then I can quickly deploy

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.340
that widget to a web page
using some HTML snippets.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:33.690
For something more advanced I'm
going to use the C Sharp SDK.

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:37.080
So I'm going to go in to
Visual Studio now and look

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:42.810
at an application that uses the
C Sharp SDK alongside Blazor

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:46.740
Server to produce a completely
custom user interface.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:51.390
To quickly retrieve an answer
from Progress Agentic RAG

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:55.170
within the SDK I can call
upon the search interface

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:57.840
and use the ask async method.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:02.670
With the ask async method I have
the option to supply a structure

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:06.180
that I would like the Progress
Agentic RAG system to fulfill.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.400
This is just a
plain class object.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:10.770
This one is called
chart augmented answer.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:16.020
And I'm going to pass that type
along with the user's request

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:19.170
and when the type is passed

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:23.400
in to the system this chart
augmented answer gets turned

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.500
in to a JSON structure.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:29.370
That JSON structure is then
seen by the retrieval agent

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:32.700
and the retrieval agent
will map the values

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:36.630
from the search results
in to the JSON structure

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:40.380
and fulfill all of the
properties that we ask for.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:43.080
That gets serialized back in to

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:47.400
that chart augmented answer
object within our application

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.090
and then I can easily display
that information on the screen

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:54.420
to the user in the form
of text and charts.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.180
Now the application is
running in the browser

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:00.660
and I have a completely
customized user interface

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:05.160
that was built using the Telerik
UI for Blazor component library

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.260
and is all backed

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:11.160
by the Progress Agentic
RAG C Sharp SDK.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:14.430
In my chat interface I
have some suggestions here

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:17.910
so I can compare Nvidia and
Google's revenue at the click

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.790
of a button or I can
enter a query just using

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.320
natural language.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.590
So when I compare Nvidia
to Google you can see

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:30.240
that I get a detailed comparison
in text, but I also have a chart

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:33.690
that I can open and when I open
the chart that structured data

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:37.380
that I asked for from Progress
Agentic RAG is relayed back

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:41.040
to my Blazor application so
it can be easily passed off

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:44.130
to some UI components for
rendering chart data.

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:47.670
This just shows the depth
of how custom you can go

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:51.630
with Progress Agentic
RAG and SDKs available

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.660
and whatever front end
technology that you like to use.

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:55.710
For more information

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:58.920
about Progress Agentic
RAG visit progress.com

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:02.280
and you can also use the QR code
shown on screen to gather all

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.930
of the resources that were found

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:07.470
in this presentation including
the financial services

